---
import ToolPageLayout from '../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro';
import FinalPayCalculator from '../islands/FinalPayCalculator';
import laborRules from '../data/labor/2026.json';

const meta = laborRules.meta;
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is final pay (back pay) in the Philippines?',
    a: 'Final pay — also called back pay or last pay — is the sum of all wages and monetary benefits still owed to you when your employment ends, whatever the cause of separation. DOLE Labor Advisory No. 06-20 treats final pay, last pay and back pay as the same concept.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should final pay be released?',
    a: 'Within 30 days from your date of separation, per DOLE Labor Advisory No. 06-20, unless a more favorable company policy, individual agreement, or CBA applies. Your Certificate of Employment must be issued within 3 days of your request.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is usually included in final pay?',
    a: 'Typical components: unpaid earned salary; the cash value of unused Service Incentive Leave (and other convertible leave); pro-rated 13th month pay; separation pay if the separation cause requires it; retirement pay if qualified; tax refund if over-withheld; and return of deposits or cash bonds. Deductions for loans and accountabilities may be subtracted.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is pro-rated 13th month pay in final pay computed?',
    a: 'Total basic salary you earned during the calendar year up to your last day, divided by 12. Example: resigned June 30 after earning ₱20,000 monthly → ₱120,000 ÷ 12 = ₱10,000 pro-rated 13th month pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I get separation pay if I resign?',
    a: 'Generally no — voluntary resignation does not entitle you to separation pay unless your company policy, contract, or CBA grants it. Separation pay is required for authorized causes such as retrenchment, redundancy, closure not due to serious losses, or disease (½ to 1 month pay per year of service depending on the cause).',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is separation pay computed?',
    a: 'It depends on the authorized cause. Redundancy or installation of labor-saving devices: one month pay per year of service. Retrenchment, closure not due to serious losses, or disease: one-half month pay per year of service. In every case the minimum is one month pay, and a fraction of at least six months counts as a full year (Labor Code Arts. 298–299). No separation pay is required for resignation, just-cause dismissal or closure due to serious losses.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I compute final pay for a resigned employee?',
    a: 'Add (1) unpaid salary for days worked since the last payroll — daily rate × days; (2) unused Service Incentive Leave and other convertible leave × daily rate; (3) pro-rated 13th month pay — basic salary earned this year ÷ 12; and (4) any tax refund or returnable deposit. Then subtract loan balances and documented accountabilities. Separation pay is not included on resignation unless policy or a CBA grants it. The employer must release the result within 30 days of your last day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is unused service incentive leave converted to cash?',
    a: 'Employees who have worked at least one year are entitled to five days of Service Incentive Leave per year (Labor Code Art. 95). Unused SIL is convertible to cash at the end of the year, and any remaining balance is paid in your final pay: unused days × your daily rate. Company vacation or sick leave beyond the five statutory days follows company policy.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Paano kinukwenta ang back pay?',
    a: 'Idagdag ang (1) hindi pa bayad na sahod para sa mga araw na pumasok ka — daily rate × araw; (2) cash conversion ng hindi nagamit na Service Incentive Leave; (3) pro-rated 13th month pay — kabuuang basic salary ngayong taon ÷ 12; at (4) tax refund kung sobra ang kaltas. Bawasin ang loan at accountabilities. Halimbawa: ₱26,100 monthly, 10 araw na hindi pa bayad (₱12,000), 5 araw na SIL (₱6,000), 8 buwang 13th month (₱17,400), less ₱2,000 loan = ₱33,400 na back pay, dapat ibigay sa loob ng 30 araw.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Gaano katagal bago makuha ang back pay?',
    a: 'Within 30 days from the date of separation or termination, unless a more favorable company policy, contract or CBA applies (DOLE Labor Advisory No. 06-20). Clearance processing does not extend the 30-day rule; if it is late, you may file a request for assistance with the nearest DOLE office.',
  },
  {
    q: 'May back pay ba kung nag-resign ako?',
    a: 'Oo. Final pay is due whether you resigned or were terminated: unpaid salary up to your last day, pro-rated 13th month pay, cash conversion of unused service incentive leave (or company leave, if policy converts it), and any other amounts due. Separation pay is the exception — it is not owed on voluntary resignation unless company policy or a CBA grants it.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Final Pay Calculator Philippines – Back Pay Computation 2026"
  description="Estimate your final pay (back pay): unpaid salary, leave conversion, pro-rated 13th month, separation pay — with the DOLE 30-day release rule."
  path="/final-pay-calculator/"
  h1="Final Pay / Back Pay Calculator Philippines"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['thirteenth', 'overtime', 'sssCalculator', 'takeHome']}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      Your final pay (back pay) is the <strong>sum of everything your employer still owes you at
      separation</strong> — unpaid salary, unused leave converted to cash, pro-rated 13th month
      pay, plus separation or retirement pay where applicable, minus outstanding deductions. Under
      DOLE Labor Advisory No. 06-20 it must be released <strong>within 30 days</strong> from your
      separation date. Fill in only the items that apply to you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="calculator">
    <FinalPayCalculator client:idle />
  </Fragment>

  <h2>What Goes Into Final Pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unpaid earned salary</strong> — days worked in your last cut-off that haven't been paid.</li>
    <li><strong>Unused Service Incentive Leave (SIL)</strong> — the 5-day statutory leave is convertible to cash if unused; company vacation leave follows company policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Pro-rated 13th month pay</strong> — total basic salary earned this calendar year ÷ 12 (auto-computed above, or see the <a href="/13th-month-pay-calculator/">13th Month Pay Calculator</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Separation pay</strong> — only for authorized-cause separations (retrenchment, redundancy, closure, disease) or when granted by policy/CBA.</li>
    <li><strong>Retirement pay</strong> — if you qualify under RA 7641 or a company plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Tax refund</strong> — if your withheld tax exceeds your annualized tax due for the year.</li>
    <li><strong>Deposits and bonds</strong> — cash bonds or deposits that must be returned.</li>
    <li><strong>Less: deductions</strong> — unpaid loans (company, SSS, Pag-IBIG salary loans routed through payroll) and documented accountabilities.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not Everyone Gets Every Item</h2>
  <p>
    Resigning employees typically receive unpaid salary, leave conversion and pro-rated 13th month
    pay — but not separation pay. Retrenched employees add separation pay. Retirees add retirement
    pay. The calculator treats every line as optional for exactly this reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Example Computations</h2>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 1 — Resigned September 30, ₱20,000/month:</strong> unpaid salary ₱10,000 +
    unused SIL ₱4,545 + pro-rated 13th month (₱180,000 ÷ 12) ₱15,000 = <strong>₱29,545</strong>
    before deductions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 2 — Retrenched after 4 years, ₱18,000/month (June 30 separation):</strong>
    separation pay for retrenchment = ½ month pay per year of service (minimum 1 month) = ₱9,000 ×
    4 = ₱36,000. Add pro-rated 13th month (₱108,000 ÷ 12) ₱9,000 and unpaid salary ₱9,000 =
    <strong>₱54,000</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 3 — With deductions:</strong> additions total ₱23,833.33 but an unpaid
    company loan of ₱1,500 is outstanding → final pay = <strong>₱22,333.33</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Separation Pay Computation (Authorized Causes)</h2>
  <p>
    Separation pay is owed only when the employer ends the employment for an <strong>authorized
    cause</strong> under Articles 298–299 of the Labor Code, or when a company policy, contract or
    CBA grants it. The rate depends on the cause, and a fraction of at least six months of service
    counts as one whole year.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Cause of separation</th><th>Separation pay</th><th>Minimum</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Redundancy; installation of labor-saving devices</td><td>1 month pay per year of service</td><td>1 month pay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Retrenchment to prevent losses; closure or cessation not due to serious losses</td><td>½ month pay per year of service</td><td>1 month pay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Disease (continued employment prohibited by law or prejudicial to health)</td><td>½ month pay per year of service</td><td>1 month pay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Closure due to serious business losses</td><td>None required by law</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Resignation; dismissal for just cause; end of a fixed-term or project contract</td><td>None required by law (policy or CBA may grant it)</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example — ₱18,000/month, 4 years and 7 months of service:</strong> service rounds
    up to 5 years. Retrenchment: ₱9,000 × 5 = <strong>₱45,000</strong>. Redundancy: ₱18,000 × 5 =
    <strong>₱90,000</strong>. Enter the figure in the “Separation pay” field above to add it to
    your final pay.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sample Final Pay Computation for a Resigned Employee</h2>
  <p>
    Resigned effective August 31; monthly basic salary ₱26,100; five-day work week (261-day
    factor); 10 working days in the last cutoff not yet paid; 5 unused Service Incentive Leave
    days; no separation pay; one outstanding company loan of ₱2,000.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Step</th><th>Computation</th><th>Amount</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1. Daily rate</td><td>₱26,100 × 12 ÷ 261</td><td>₱1,200.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Unpaid salary</td><td>₱1,200 × 10 days</td><td>₱12,000.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Unused SIL converted to cash</td><td>₱1,200 × 5 days</td><td>₱6,000.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. Pro-rated 13th month pay</td><td>₱26,100 × 8 months ÷ 12</td><td>₱17,400.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. Gross final pay</td><td>Steps 2 + 3 + 4</td><td>₱35,400.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6. Less deductions</td><td>Company loan balance</td><td>− ₱2,000.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Net final pay</strong></td><td>Due within 30 days of August 31</td><td><strong>₱33,400.00</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
    Withholding tax on the final payout is annualized by the employer; if too much tax was withheld
    during the year, the refund is added to this amount. The same steps apply to terminated
    employees, with separation pay added where the cause requires it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who This Applies To</h2>
  <p>
    Any private-sector employee whose employment ended — resignation, end of contract,
    retrenchment, redundancy, dismissal, or retirement. The composition varies by cause of
    separation; the 30-day release rule applies to all. This result is an estimate: the exact
    figure depends on your employer's payroll records and clearance process, and disputes can be
    raised with DOLE.
  </p>

  <h2>Official Source</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>DOLE Labor Advisory No. 06-20</strong> (Guidelines on the Payment of Final Pay and
    Issuance of Certificate of Employment), and the
    <a href={meta.official_source_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOLE Handbook on Workers' Statutory Monetary Benefits</a>.
    Last verified {new Date(meta.last_verified + 'T00:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-PH', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric' })}.
  </p>
</ToolPageLayout>
